WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:03.940
Hey there Crazies.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.640
In our video about the universe being a big
simulation, we said this.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.860
What about Planck time?

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.460
and John...

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:18.620
John

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.320
He was wondering what the heck Planck units were.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.320
First things first.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.300
What are units?!

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:28.940
Units are really important!

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:32.160
We can sit and theorize all day but,
eventually, we’re going to have to take

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.840
some measurements in the real world.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.260
and that demands a unit!

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.380
Which could really be anything as we saw in
the faking gravity video:

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:39.920
It's about 5 football fields,

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.920
or 3 and a half Giza pyramids,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.700
or 110 Volkswagen Jettas,

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.800
or 733 tube socks!

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.200
It doesn’t matter what the unit is,
just that we all agree and understand

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:50.860
what it is.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.040
Otherwise, you end up with a Mars Polar Lander
crashing into Mars

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.040
because someone didn’t do a unit conversion.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.740
Seriously, links in the doobly-doo.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.380
Actually, it matters a little.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.830
The metric system is based on 10’s which is

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:06.120
significantly easier to use than the imperial
system used in the United States.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.820
[Groaning Noises]

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:09.200
So here’s the problem.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.680
What if you wanted to communicate the average
height of a human being to an alien race?

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.450
[Alien Noises]
Yes, Milton, like you.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.720
They’re not going to understand football
fields or tube socks

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.400
or any abstract units like miles or meters.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.020
You need something you know that they are
familiar with.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:29.760
We call these types of units "natural units"
because they’re based on nature.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.640
They’re supposed to be independent of our
subjectivity.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.280
You know, that stuff we impose on almost everything.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.120
For example, I could measure my height
in ground-state hydrogen atoms.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.720
All of which are identically about 1/10 of
a nano-meter tall.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.000
We sometimes call that an Angstrom after the
Swedish physicist.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.000
That would make me about 18 billion hydrogen
atoms tall.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.300
That's pretty average for a human.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.540
This is kind of what Planck Units are, but
instead of being based on ideas like atoms,

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:58.380
they’re based on the fundamental constants
of nature.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:02.100
The gravitational constant, which dictates
the influence of gravity.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.360
Planck’s constant, which determines how
lumpy and uncertain the quantum world is.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:10.040
The Speed of Light, which is how quickly different
parts of space-time can communicate.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:12.420
Coulomb’s constant, which governs electricity.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:16.800
And Boltzmann’s constant, which controls
the statistical behavior of large systems

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:17.820
of particles.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.060
We’re pretty sure they’re the same everyone
in the universe.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.380
But, again, aliens are going to have
their own units

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.590
and, if they have a different number of these,
they’re not going to use base-10 math either.

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:30.020
To make these more universal, we need to do
something crazy.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:34.180
Just for the sake of argument, let’s set
all of these equal to one.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:38.510
The number one exists in all base-systems
and there are no more units.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:40.360
Wait ,how can you just do that?

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.740
Well, you can’t, unless you change everything
else too.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.260
Changing those constants to one, changes what
units we use for:

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:52.620
Length, Time, Mass, Charge, Temperature,
and, frankly, any other units based on those.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:57.900
Area, Volume, Force, Energy, Power, Momentum,
Frequency

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.100
The list goes on!

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:02.700
Every single one of those is now measured
in what we call a “Planck Unit”

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:06.460
named for the German physicist Max Karl Ernst
Ludwig Planck,

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:11.240
or just Max Planck for short, who invented
most of them back in 1900 CE.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:14.680
Historically, understanding physical quantities
starts with length,

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:17.840
so it was very natural for Planck to do the
same with his units.

00:03:17.849 --> 00:03:20.959
Plank Length is about 10 to the negative 35th meters.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.160
To understand how small this is, we need
to turn back the clock a little.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.260
To the time machine!

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:32.680
Playing the expanding universe in reverse,
we can look way back in time.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.270
At one point, the universe was the size of
a basketball,

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:39.100
and then a human egg cell, and then an atom,
and then a proton.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:44.900
At 10 to the negative 18 meters, we reach
the smallest distance we’ve ever measured.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.460
Planck length is 17 more decimal places!

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:49.860
It’s insane!

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.400
When the universe was this small, we call
that the Planck Era.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.120
It was one Planck length across,
one Planck time old,

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:00.320
and one Planck temperature hot, which is really hot!

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.060
OK, let’s get back to the lab!

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:03.730
What’s so special about Planck length?

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:04.730
Nothing really.

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:05.600
At least, not yet.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.400
Some models of quantum gravity say that Planck
length is the smallest possible distance.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.400
Because quantum mechanics loves it when things
are quantized like that.

00:04:13.409 --> 00:04:17.829
But remember, we’ve only been able to measure
down to 10 to the negative 18 meters,

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:20.020
so we’re not really sure about anything
smaller.

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:23.600
And no model of quantum gravity has experimental
evidence anyway.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.420
Even if space has a smallest amount of chunk
or whatever.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:31.300
It could just as easily be a 10th of the Planck
length or 1000 times the Plank length,

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:34.320
as long as it’s less than 10 to the negative
18 meters.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.339
What I find interesting is how ordinary some
of the other Planck units are.

00:04:38.339 --> 00:04:40.910
Planck Mass is about the mass of a flea egg.

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:45.189
Planck Energy is about what we could get out
of a 16 gallon tank of gasoline.

00:04:45.189 --> 00:04:49.300
Planck Resistance is about 30 ohms, which
is resistor I use all the time.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.900
That’s kinda cool.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.420
So, do you think the Planck units have any
physical significance?

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.639
Let’s continue this conversation in the
comments.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:57.039
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.020
Please subscribe if you’d like
to see more science like this.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:03.240
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:06.400
In the last video a clone fell into a black
hole.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:07.680
Comment response time!

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:11.960
Amit Patel was wondering why larger black
holes don’t spaghettify you like smaller

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:12.780
ones do.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.979
An event horizon is an event horizon, right?!

00:05:14.979 --> 00:05:18.499
Yes, an event horizon is always a place of
infinite time dilation

00:05:18.499 --> 00:05:21.589
and yes, bigger black holes have stronger
gravity,

00:05:21.589 --> 00:05:25.020
but that’s okay because the strength of
gravity isn’t what rips you apart.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.420
It’s the difference in strength between,
say, your head and your feet.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:32.219
Those differences are bigger around smaller
black holes even though,

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:33.840
overall, those holes are weaker.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:37.919
Kamikaze Katorin and DavidCH12345 asked what
the rest of the universe would look like to

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:40.440
the expendable clone as he fell into the black
hole.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:44.660
At first, I thought maybe the entire future
of the universe would pass before his eyes,

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:46.080
but that’s not actually the case.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:49.560
Even though the rest of the universe does
speed up from his point of view,

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.530
it doesn’t speed up enough for him to see
the end of the universe.

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:56.160
The last light he sees is not the last light
that arrives at the horizon.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:57.640
Infinity is so weird!

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:01.440
Johan was thinking maybe the event horizon
and the singularity are equivalent.

00:06:01.449 --> 00:06:03.439
Mathematically, that’s not that far off.

00:06:03.439 --> 00:06:07.080
A singularity is just a place where an equation
blows up to infinity.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:10.550
The black hole singularity is what you’d
call a physical singularity.

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:12.740
It’s there no matter what your point of
view is.

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:15.979
The event horizon is what you’d call
a coordinate singularity.

00:06:15.979 --> 00:06:20.570
It’s there in one set of coordinates,
like the ones I'd use on my space station watching at

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:23.060
a safe distance,
but not another set of coordinates,

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:26.080
like the ones my clone would use as he's falling into the black hole.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.540
So they're very similar mathematically, but physically
they are very different things.

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:33.759
If you’re looking for a more thorough conversation
about how Hawking Radiation might affect the

00:06:33.759 --> 00:06:37.770
fall into the black hole,
the comment thread started by david21686 is

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:38.770
a good place to start.

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.520
Otherwise, you’ll have to wait until I’m
ready to do a video on the Black Hole Information

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:42.520
Paradox.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:44.520
It’s tough stuff, so it’s taking a while.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.240
And, finally, Your Master seems to want to start a clone

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.220
union to protect all my clones from all the science we do here.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:51.360
That’s no fun!

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:54.660
I’m not entirely sure how I feel about this,
but good luck getting them to organize.

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:57.120
I have a hard enough time and they’re my
clones.

